    <div class="max-w-3xl mx-auto px-4 pb-16 lg:pb-24">
      <div class="prose prose-lg prose-blue max-w-none text-gray-700 leading-relaxed">
        <p class="text-2xl font-medium text-gray-900 mb-8 italic">
          Treadmill delivery comes in four broad tiers — curbside, threshold, room of choice and
          white glove — and the difference between them is who carries a very heavy crate the last
          fifty feet. Choosing the right one is mostly a question of stairs, helpers and building
          rules, and it is worth settling before you order rather than on the doorstep.
        </p>

        <div id="short-answer" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">1. Treadmill Delivery Options: The Short Answer</h2>
<p>
            <strong>Curbside</strong> means the crate is left at the end of your driveway or at the
            building entrance, and everything after that is your job. <strong>Threshold</strong>
            means it is brought to or just inside your front door. <strong>Room of choice</strong>
            means the crew carries it to the room you name. <strong>White glove</strong> normally
            means room of choice plus assembly and removal of the packaging, and sometimes a quick
            functional check before the crew leaves.
          </p>
          <p>
            As a rough guide, upgrading from curbside to a fully assembled white-glove service
            commonly adds somewhere between $150 and $400 to the order, with threshold and
            room-of-choice options sitting below that. Specialty fitness dealers include more of
            this in the price more often than general online retailers do.
          </p>
          <p>
            <strong>Which do you need?</strong> If the treadmill is going anywhere other than a
            ground-floor room near the entrance, or if you do not have a second strong adult to help,
            room of choice is the minimum worth paying for. If you would also rather not spend an
            afternoon on <a href="/how-to-assemble-a-treadmill/" class="text-[#0F62FE] font-medium">assembly</a>,
            white glove is usually the best-value line on the invoice. Curbside only makes sense for
            a lighter machine, a short level route and two capable people standing by.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="delivery-tiers" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">2. Curbside, Threshold, Room of Choice and White Glove</h2>
<p>
            The names vary between sellers and carriers, so treat these as descriptions of what
            happens rather than as guaranteed terms.
          </p>
          <p>
            <strong>Curbside (or kerbside).</strong> A freight truck, often with a liftgate, lowers
            the crate to the ground at the nearest point it can reach — your driveway, the street
            outside a house, or the loading area of an apartment building. The driver is generally
            not obliged to move it further, and often is not insured to. This is the default for a
            great many online treadmill orders, including many that advertise free delivery.
          </p>
          <p>
            <strong>Threshold.</strong> The crate comes to your front door, a garage, or just inside
            the first doorway. Useful if the street is a long carry from the door; little help if
            the treadmill is going upstairs, because the stairs are still yours.
          </p>
          <p>
            <strong>Room of choice.</strong> Two people carry the boxed machine into the room you
            specify, including up or down stairs, usually within limits set out in the service
            terms. The box stays boxed, and the packaging stays with you.
          </p>
          <p>
            <strong>White glove.</strong> The most complete tier. The crew brings the machine into
            the room, unpacks it, assembles it, and takes the cardboard, foam and pallet away. Some
            services also power the machine up to show it runs. Assembly and packaging removal are
            the two things to confirm are actually included, because some sellers use the phrase
            loosely for what is really room of choice.
          </p>
          <p>
            There are also hybrid arrangements: room of choice with assembly but no packaging
            removal, or assembly booked separately through a third party after a curbside drop. Our
            <a href="/treadmills-for-sale/" class="text-[#0F62FE] font-medium">where to buy guide</a>
            covers which kinds of seller tend to include which.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="costs" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">3. What Each Delivery Tier Typically Costs</h2>
<p>
            These are typical ranges for a standard home treadmill in the US, not quotes. They move
            with region, machine size, distance from the carrier's depot and the time of year.
          </p>
          <ul>
            <li><strong>Curbside:</strong> often included in the price; sometimes a flat freight
            charge on lower-priced machines or remote addresses.</li>
            <li><strong>Threshold:</strong> where offered as a paid upgrade, commonly in the region
            of $50 to $150 above curbside.</li>
            <li><strong>Room of choice:</strong> commonly around $100 to $200 above curbside.</li>
            <li><strong>White glove with assembly and packaging removal:</strong> commonly around
            $150 to $400 above curbside in total, with the top of the range for heavy non-folding
            machines, incline trainers and difficult access.</li>
          </ul>
          <p>
            Surcharges are where the quoted price and the final price part company. Look for
            per-flight stair charges, long-carry charges where the truck cannot park close to the
            entrance, remote-area fees, and charges for a failed delivery if nobody is home or the
            building refuses access. A missed delivery can mean paying for the attempt and then
            paying again for the rebooking.
          </p>
          <p>
            Promotional periods change the arithmetic. Retailers frequently include upgraded delivery
            or assembly rather than cutting the headline price further, and a machine that costs
            slightly more with white glove included can be the cheaper purchase once you price the
            alternative. Our guide to
            <a href="/how-much-does-a-treadmill-cost/" class="text-[#0F62FE] font-medium">what a treadmill costs</a>
            puts delivery alongside the other extras that belong in the total, and our
            <a href="/best-time-of-year-to-buy-a-treadmill/" class="text-[#0F62FE] font-medium">price calendar</a>
            explains when those bundled offers tend to appear.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="which-tier" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">4. Which Delivery Tier You Actually Need</h2>
<p>
            Start with weight, because it rules out more options than anything else. A boxed home
            treadmill commonly weighs somewhere between about 150 and 350 lb depending on size, and
            the crate has no handles. Our guide to
            <a href="/how-much-does-a-treadmill-weigh/" class="text-[#0F62FE] font-medium">how much a treadmill weighs</a>
            gives typical boxed figures by type. A walking pad is a different proposition — light
            enough that curbside is usually fine.
          </p>
          <p>
            Then ask four questions:
          </p>
          <ul>
            <li><strong>Are there stairs anywhere on the route?</strong> Even three or four steps up
            to a front door turn a curbside drop into a lift. A full flight inside the house makes
            room of choice close to essential.</li>
            <li><strong>How many capable helpers do you have on delivery day?</strong> Two strong
            adults is the realistic minimum for a full-size boxed machine on level ground, and more
            for stairs.</li>
            <li><strong>Do you want to assemble it?</strong> If not, white glove usually costs less
            than booking delivery and an assembler separately, and it puts one company in charge if
            something goes wrong.</li>
            <li><strong>What happens to the packaging?</strong> A treadmill crate produces a
            surprising volume of cardboard, foam and sometimes a wooden pallet. If your building or
            municipal collection will not take it easily, packaging removal is worth something.</li>
          </ul>
          <p>
            If you answered yes to stairs and have fewer than two helpers, the decision is made:
            room of choice at minimum. The money saved on a curbside drop does not survive a dented
            wall, a damaged machine or a strained back.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="measure-route" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">5. Measure the Route for the Box, Not the Machine</h2>
<p>
            Before you order, get the boxed dimensions from the listing or the seller, and compare
            them with every pinch point between the truck and the room: the front door, internal
            doors, hallway turns, the stairwell and any landing. The crate is often longer and less
            forgiving than the assembled machine, because it cannot be folded, tilted around a newel
            post or partly dismantled.
          </p>
          <p>
            Measure door openings with the door fully open, since the door itself eats into the
            clear width. Stair turns are the usual problem, because a long rigid box has to pivot
            through them diagonally. If a turn looks marginal, tape the crate's footprint out on the
            floor or cut a cardboard template and walk it round first.
          </p>
          <p>
            We will not repeat the full route-planning method here: our guide to
            <a href="/how-to-move-a-treadmill/" class="text-[#0F62FE] font-medium">moving a treadmill</a>
            covers measuring pinch points, stair turns and protecting floors in detail, and the
            same principles apply to a crate arriving as to a machine leaving. Our
            <a href="/treadmill-dimensions-space-requirements/" class="text-[#0F62FE] font-medium">space requirements guide</a>
            covers the room itself, including the clearance behind the deck.
          </p>
          <p>
            Tell the seller about anything unusual when you book: a spiral staircase, a narrow
            basement door, a long gravel drive, a gated community. A crew that knows in advance
            arrives with the right number of people and equipment. A crew that discovers it on the
            day may decline the job, and the terms usually let them.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="stairs" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">6. Stairs, Walk-Ups and Basements</h2>
<p>
            Stairs are where delivery terms matter most and are read least. Room-of-choice and
            white-glove services usually include some stairs, but the details vary: some include
            one flight and charge for each additional flight, some cap the total number of steps,
            and some exclude spiral or very narrow staircases altogether. Basements count too, and
            a steep basement stair is often harder than a straight upward flight.
          </p>
          <p>
            Read the stair clause before booking, and describe your stairs honestly when asked. If
            the crew arrives to find more flights than the order says, you may be asked to pay the
            difference on the spot or the delivery may stop at the bottom of the stairs.
          </p>
          <p>
            For a walk-up apartment, consider the folded or partly assembled size as well as the
            boxed one. A few sellers will unbox at the entrance and carry components up separately
            when the crate will not make a turn; others will not. Ask before the day, not during it.
          </p>
          <p>
            If you are taking a curbside delivery and moving the box upstairs yourselves, our
            <a href="/how-to-move-a-treadmill/" class="text-[#0F62FE] font-medium">moving guide</a>
            covers the technique, the number of people, and why the downhill person carries the
            most weight. The short version is that a boxed treadmill on a staircase is a two-person
            job at the very least, and a three-person job if you want a spare pair of hands for
            doors and turns.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="apartments" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">7. Apartment Buildings: Elevators, Loading Docks and COIs</h2>
<p>
            Apartment and condo buildings add a layer of rules that the seller does not control and
            often does not know about. Find out what your building requires before you choose a
            delivery date.
          </p>
          <p>
            <strong>Elevator booking.</strong> Many buildings require large deliveries to use a
            service elevator, booked in advance for a fixed window, sometimes with protective
            padding installed by the building. Book a window that comfortably covers the carrier's
            delivery window, not just the time you hope they will arrive.
          </p>
          <p>
            <strong>Loading docks and delivery hours.</strong> Buildings commonly restrict large
            deliveries to particular entrances and hours, and some do not allow them at weekends.
            A carrier who arrives outside those hours may be turned away.
          </p>
          <p>
            <strong>Certificates of insurance.</strong> Many managed buildings ask for a certificate
            of insurance, usually shortened to COI, before they will let a delivery or installation
            crew into the building. It is a document from the delivery company's insurer showing
            that they carry liability cover, and buildings often specify the wording and the names
            that must appear on it. Ask your building management for their exact requirements in
            writing, then ask the seller whether their delivery partner can supply a COI in that
            form. Allow several working days, because it often has to be requested from the insurer.
            Curbside freight carriers generally do not supply one, since they are not entering the
            building, which is one more reason room of choice or white glove suits apartments.
          </p>
          <p>
            <strong>Doormen and concierges.</strong> Many will not sign for or store a freight item
            on your behalf. Plan to be present. And check your lease or building rules on exercise
            equipment generally — our
            <a href="/best-treadmills-for-apartments/" class="text-[#0F62FE] font-medium">apartment treadmill guide</a>
            covers noise and floor rules that are worth knowing before the machine is upstairs.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="scheduling" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">8. Scheduling, Delivery Windows and the Day Before</h2>
<p>
            Freight delivery is rarely as precise as parcel delivery. Expect a call or message to
            schedule a day, then a window on that day that may be several hours long. If the
            treadmill is shipped by a freight carrier and handed to a local delivery agent for the
            final leg, there may be a gap of days between it arriving at the depot and being offered
            for delivery.
          </p>
          <p>
            The day before, do the boring things that make the delivery go smoothly:
          </p>
          <ul>
            <li>Clear the route, including rugs, shoe racks, pictures on stairwell walls and
            anything a crew walking backwards could catch.</li>
            <li>Clear and measure the final spot, and decide which way round the machine will face
            so assembly happens in place.</li>
            <li>Check there is a suitable wall outlet within reach. Treadmill manuals almost
            universally prohibit extension cords, and our
            <a href="/treadmill-surge-protector/" class="text-[#0F62FE] font-medium">surge protector guide</a>
            explains what to plug into.</li>
            <li>Have the order confirmation, the delivery terms and your phone to hand for photos.</li>
            <li>Put down the mat if you have one, so the machine is built on it rather than lifted
            onto it later.</li>
          </ul>
          <p>
            If you cannot be home for the full window, reschedule in advance rather than hoping. A
            failed delivery attempt is often chargeable, and freight rebooking can add a week or
            more.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="inspecting" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">9. Inspecting the Delivery Before You Sign</h2>
<p>
            The delivery receipt — sometimes called the proof of delivery, or for freight the bill
            of lading — is the most important piece of paper in this process. What you write on it,
            or fail to write, shapes how easily any damage claim is accepted later.
          </p>
          <p>
            <strong>Inspect the crate before signing.</strong> Walk round it. Look for crushed
            corners, punctures, forklift damage, tears, water staining, and anything that rattles
            when it should not. Photograph every side, including the label, before it is moved
            further.
          </p>
          <p>
            <strong>Write specific damage on the receipt.</strong> If anything is wrong, describe it
            on the delivery receipt before you sign, in plain terms: "box crushed at rear left
            corner", "puncture on top face", "pallet broken". A specific note carries more weight
            than a generic one, and signing a clean receipt is often treated as accepting the
            delivery in good condition. Ask the driver to sign or acknowledge the note if they will.
          </p>
          <p>
            <strong>With white glove, inspect the machine itself.</strong> Because the crew unpacks
            and assembles, you can check the frame, deck, console screen and covers before they
            leave. Ask them to power it up and run the belt briefly at low speed. Check the console
            lights, the belt moves smoothly, the incline responds if it has one, and nothing grinds.
            Do this before the packaging is taken away, because the packaging may be needed if
            something has to go back.
          </p>
          <p>
            <strong>Keep everything.</strong> Photos, the receipt copy, the order confirmation and
            the names of anyone you spoke to. If the machine arrives on curbside delivery, keep the
            packaging until you have assembled and run it, at least for the seller's return window.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="damaged" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">10. What to Do If the Treadmill Arrives Damaged</h2>
<p>
            The right response depends on whether the damage is visible at the door or only found
            once the box is open.
          </p>
          <p>
            <strong>Obvious damage at delivery.</strong> If the crate is badly damaged — crushed,
            visibly broken, the frame showing through — you may have the option of refusing the
            delivery so that it goes back to the seller as a carrier problem rather than yours.
            Check the seller's delivery terms first if you can, because some prefer you to accept
            with a damage note and let them send parts. Either way, photograph it and annotate the
            receipt.
          </p>
          <p>
            <strong>Concealed damage.</strong> Damage found after unpacking — a cracked console, a
            bent upright, a gouged deck — should be reported to the seller as soon as you find it.
            Many sellers and carriers set short windows for reporting concealed damage, sometimes
            only a few days from delivery, so do not leave the box in the hall for a fortnight.
            Photograph the damage, the packaging around it, and the box label.
          </p>
          <p>
            <strong>Contact the seller, not just the carrier.</strong> Your contract is with the
            seller, and they generally manage the claim with the carrier. Ask what they want:
            replacement parts, a replacement machine, or a return. For minor cosmetic damage a parts
            shipment is the fastest fix; for frame, deck or motor damage, push for a replacement.
          </p>
          <p>
            <strong>Do not use a machine that might be structurally damaged.</strong> A bent frame
            or a cracked deck is a safety issue, not a cosmetic one. Our guide to
            <a href="/treadmill-return-policies/" class="text-[#0F62FE] font-medium">treadmill return policies</a>
            covers how returns typically work if it comes to that, and our
            <a href="/treadmill-warranty-guide/" class="text-[#0F62FE] font-medium">warranty guide</a>
            covers faults that only appear after the first few weeks.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="after-delivery" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">11. After the Crew Leaves: Packaging, Assembly and First Run</h2>
<p>
            If you took curbside, threshold or room-of-choice delivery, the machine is now a boxed
            kit in your house. Our
            <a href="/how-to-assemble-a-treadmill/" class="text-[#0F62FE] font-medium">assembly guide</a>
            covers the job itself — tools, the two-person lift out of the box, the console cable that
            catches most people out, and the order to tighten bolts in — so we will not repeat it
            here.
          </p>
          <p>
            If you took white glove, there are still a few checks the crew may not do. Confirm the
            machine is level and does not rock. Check the belt is centred and runs without drifting;
            our <a href="/how-to-center-treadmill-belt/" class="text-[#0F62FE] font-medium">belt centring guide</a>
            covers the adjustment if it wanders. Find the safety key and confirm it stops the belt.
            Register the warranty while the paperwork is in front of you.
          </p>
          <p>
            Decide what to do with the packaging. If it has not been taken away, breaking down a
            crate takes longer than people expect, and some municipal collections limit the volume
            of cardboard per pickup. Keep it, folded flat, until the seller's return period has
            passed if you have anywhere to store it; a treadmill without its original packaging can
            be much harder to return.
          </p>
          <p>
            Finally, walk on it before you run on it. A few minutes at low speed is a sensible first
            session on any new machine, and it is the moment any delivery or assembly problem tends
            to announce itself.
          </p>
        </div>
      </div>


      <div class="prose prose-lg prose-blue max-w-none text-gray-700 leading-relaxed">

        <!-- FAQ -->
        <div id="faq" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">12. Frequently Asked Questions</h2>
          <div class="space-y-6">
            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">What is white glove delivery for a treadmill?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                White glove delivery usually means the crew carries the treadmill into the room you choose, unpacks it, assembles it and takes the packaging away, sometimes with a brief power-on check. The term is used loosely, so confirm in writing that assembly and packaging removal are both included rather than assuming.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">How much does treadmill delivery cost?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                Curbside delivery is often included in the price. As typical US ranges, threshold upgrades commonly add around $50 to $150, room of choice around $100 to $200, and full white glove with assembly and packaging removal around $150 to $400 above curbside. Stair, long-carry and remote-area surcharges can add more.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">Will treadmill delivery bring it upstairs?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                Only if you pay for a tier that includes it. Curbside and threshold delivery stop at the street or the door. Room-of-choice and white-glove services usually include some stairs, but many limit the number of flights or charge per additional flight, and some exclude spiral or very narrow staircases. Read the stair clause before booking.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">What is a COI for treadmill delivery?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                A certificate of insurance is a document from a delivery company's insurer showing it carries liability cover. Many apartment and condo buildings require one before a crew can enter. Get your building's exact requirements in writing, ask the seller early whether their delivery partner can provide it, and allow several working days.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">What should I do if my treadmill is delivered damaged?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                Photograph the crate before it moves, write specific damage on the delivery receipt before signing, and contact the seller straight away. For concealed damage found after unpacking, report it as soon as you find it, because reporting windows can be short. Keep the packaging, and do not use a machine with a bent frame or cracked deck.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">Is white glove delivery worth it for a treadmill?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                Usually, if the treadmill is going upstairs, you do not have a second strong adult to help, or you would rather not assemble it. White glove often costs less than booking delivery and an assembler separately, and it leaves one company responsible if something is damaged on the way in.
              </p>
            </div>
          </div>
        </div>

        <!-- Common Mistakes -->
        <div class="mt-16">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">Common Mistakes With Treadmill Delivery</h2>
          <p class="text-gray-700 leading-relaxed">Most delivery problems are decided at the checkout page, not on the doorstep. These are the four that cost people the most.</p>
          <div class="space-y-6 my-8">
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Assuming free delivery means it comes inside</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">"Free delivery" on a treadmill very often means curbside: a crate weighing as much as an adult left at the end of the driveway. Read the tier description, not the headline, and price the upgrade before comparing sellers.</p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Measuring the machine instead of the box</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">The crate cannot fold, tilt or be partly dismantled, and it is often longer than the assembled deck. Compare the boxed dimensions with every door and stair turn on the route, and tell the seller about tight spots when you book.</p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Forgetting the building</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">Service elevator bookings, delivery hours and certificate-of-insurance requirements are set by your building, not the seller. A crew turned away at the lobby can mean a failed-delivery fee and a week-long wait for a new slot.</p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Signing a clean receipt over a damaged box</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">Signing without noting visible damage is often treated as accepting the delivery in good condition. Photograph the crate, write specific damage on the receipt before signing, and keep the packaging until you have run the machine.</p>
            </div>
          </div>
        </div>

